## 1. Design, impact and operational boundaries

### 1.1 R09 — decode safely without changing historical identity

Decode the five predefined XML entities and valid decimal/hex numeric character references in attributes/text **before URL validation and identity calculation**. Reject invalid scalar values/malformed input according to explicit parser policy. No DTD expansion, external entity resolution or new network requests. Prefer a small tested helper in the existing module; add a parser dependency only if correctness cannot be achieved safely and after revising the impact plan.

Existing GUID-less feeds used raw URL text for fallback identity. Corrected decoding therefore must resolve both raw/normalized identity aliases against existing episodes/importKeys before creating anything. Preserve existing episode document IDs, counters, publication flags and audit history; reserve owned aliases to the same ID transactionally. Conflicting aliases stop for review; never merge distinct episodes automatically. Already-bad URLs get a separate reviewed bounded repair, not a broad reimport. Repair must not create/delete episodes, toggle publication, increment parent counts, or emit real publication notifications.

### 1.2 R11 — bounded fair progress

Preserve sequential station probing and the shared maximum of four outbound fetches per instance; do not bypass `public-fetch.js` or its8-second deadline. **Proposed budget:**100 seconds per root, at least40 seconds reserved for flushing/cleanup within the existing300-second invocation limit. Before starting a primary+backup pair, require at least16 seconds plus a measured flush margin. If a future timeout changes, derive budget from the same constants.

Persist a private per-root cursor, lease/generation and last completed station ID. **Proposed path:** `{root}/streamHealthJobs/checkpoints/active`; clients of every role cannot read/write it. Stable document-ID paging must progress for >100 stations and survive station deletion; skip missing records without recreating them. Concurrent invocations cannot both advance/overwrite progress. Keep zero writes to stable station documents under the existing heartbeat policy; checkpoint writes are a separately documented bounded operational exception, not disguised as zero total writes. Inject a clock/probe for deterministic budget tests.

### 1.3 R12 — one count authority

**Proposed module:** `functions/lib/comment-counts.js`; **proposed private ledger:** `{root}/commentCountContributions/episodes/{episodeId}/comments/{commentId}`. Store only contribution/revision/state needed for correctness, no comment body/name/email. No client role may read/write the ledger or directly change counters.

One transaction protocol reads the current comment, episode and prior contribution before writes, calculates the delta between desired/current published contribution and ledger, and updates count/ledger atomically. Event retries/out-of-order events recompute from fresh current state instead of trusting event snapshots. Manual moderation passes its authorized intended transition through the same authority in the same transaction. Account deletion uses the same protocol when removing comments; remove the competing standalone decrement/recount-overwrite path. Never recreate a deleted episode/comment. Define deleted ledger cleanup and delayed-event handling in T01; active contributions cannot expire arbitrarily.

Backfill must initialize ledger and count consistently under a scoped write-quiescence window or a tested revision barrier. A plain aggregate count followed by overwrite is not sufficient. **Proposed limit:**25 items per invocation, explicit episode/root, resumable checkpoint, private before-images and conditional rollback of ledger/counter only—never restore deleted comments or overwrite current moderation. Large episodes require bounded pages plus a final revision-checked activation; no partial active baseline.

### 1.4 R13 — policy decision and delivery-preserving cutover

Preferred approved semantics: eligibility weekday/minute/local date and operation key use each schedule's accepted UTC offset. Revalidate the schedule immediately before starting send; a concurrent schedule edit must not mutate an already-bound intent. Retain claim/start/finish, pending/failed/sent/unknown behavior; **unknown never auto-retries**.

Pause old/new workers during the key/date cutover, select a documented cutover day/window, and map old Yemen-date receipts to new local occurrences conservatively. Existing terminal sent/unknown receipts suppress duplicates, including ambiguous cross-midnight overlap. Do not delete receipts or reset lastNotifiedDate to force sends. Where mapping is ambiguous, suppress and require operator review; skipped historical delivery is safer than unauthorized repeat delivery. Rollback pauses workers and preserves both old/new receipt evidence; it does not blindly restore the old key scheme.

### 1.5 Blast radius

High: shared entry point, private state, counters, delivery identity, contracts, maintenance tools and emulator harness. All writes are enumerated below; no new packages or lockfiles planned. Any necessary index update is additive and reviewed by the shared integration owner after WO-03. Later production work is separately authorized; this planning request executes none of it.

