## 3. Pre-execution checklist and migration safeguards

- Recheck current commit, user edits, AGENTS, contracts and worker definitions; no dispatch on stale evidence.
- Approve R13 policy/ADR and count-ledger baseline/cleanup rules before code. No silent change from YemenDate.
- Compute write-set union and authorize high-impact implementation according to current user authorization. This document alone grants none.
- Every migration proposed above requires explicit project/one root/scope, dry-run default, deterministic reviewed manifest with schema/version/hash and expected source fingerprint, durable private backup verified before mutation, batches of at most25 transactions/concurrency1 by default, resumable lease/checkpoint and conditional restore.
- Backups live outside Git and `.boss`, contain minimum necessary restricted data, have least-privilege encrypted storage and defined retention. Reports contain counts/status/hashes only, no URLs/tokens/comment content/email.
- Wrong project/root, missing backup, source change, ownership conflict, unknown schema or lost lease stops mutation. No broad delete/update or automatic merge of conflicting identities.
- RSS rollback cannot create/delete/re-publish episodes; count rollback cannot restore comments or stale moderation; notification rollback cannot reset sent/unknown receipts or resend. Health rollback pauses worker and preserves cursor evidence.
- All rollback tooling must preserve WO-01 privacy changes: never restore private station fields to public documents or email to listener profiles from older backups.
- Retain existing authentication, deletion barriers, delivery privacy and public-fetch SSRF/deadline protections. No real Gemini/Telegram/email/FCM as an implicit verification step.

## 4. Dependency graph and Evidence Waves

### 4.0 Optimized dispatch authority — retain atomic tasks, reduce handoffs

The optimized [master execution plan](execution-plan.md) and [runtime waves](waves.json) own dispatch batching and scheduling. Atomic task IDs, write sets, acceptance criteria, security controls, regressions, migration limits and independent-review gates in this document remain mandatory traceability units; they are **not** instructions to spawn an agent per task. A batch owner retains context across its included tasks and executes their true prerequisites locally. No finding is CLOSED by batching or by an interim patch handoff.

**Every repeated path has exactly one content owner in `waves.json.sharedFileOwners`.** Of33 registered paths,22 cross-batch/control paths remain exclusively owned by I;11 wholly intra-batch paths stay with their designated stable component worker. I retains `functions/index.js`, Rules/Rules tests, cross-batch relationship modules, the emulator harness, shared contracts/operational documents and shared admin paths according to that exact registry. Specialists prepare patches for I-owned paths without competing working-tree writes. For delegated intra-batch paths, the same component owner writes across its atomic steps without an intermediate I handoff. I integrates the reviewed batch commit without independent content edits; conflicts return to the designated content owner. This ownership refinement changes neither task scope nor acceptance criteria and never permits concurrent writers.

Code/contract handoff is an INTERIM availability event, not WO closure, QA acceptance or a passed Evidence Wave. Co-located count/worker integration and cross-component test work is completed before final acceptance, and all17 previously skipped admin emulator checks must actually run and pass after the real handler is registered. Independent reviewer and QA approvals remain after integrated GREEN, on the exact candidate commit. Manual policy, device, rehearsal and production checkpoints are separate from executable code-wave RED/GREEN; never fabricate RED for an already-correct deny rule.

| Legacy section label | Runtime alias / checkpoint |
|---|---|
| W1 T01 | Manual policy/ADR gate; not an executable RED/GREEN wave |
| W2 T02–T03 | `W04-W2` |
| W3 T04–T05 | `W04-W3` |
| W4 T06–T09 | `W04-W4` |
| W5 T10–T11 | `W04-W5` |
| W6 T12–T15 | Executable integration/authorization portion: `W04-W6-CODE`; independent review and operational rehearsal remain separate manual checkpoints |
| W7 T16 | Separately authorized production checkpoint; not executable code-wave RED |

`W04-W6-CODE` retains pre-existing private-path denial as baseline/guard GREEN. It is a **guard-only verification wave**, expected to PASS at baseline and after integration; no separate defect RED is required for that wave. Intentional business RED remains in the authored count/real-worker regression of `W04-W4` and timing/receipt regression of `W04-W5`, not in a sabotaged permission rule. The runtime artifact distinguishes those defect assertions from invariant guards.

In this WO, BE retains `functions/lib/stream-health.js` throughout B14 (04-T04/04-T05), preserving one worker context without an intermediate I content-edit handoff. `functions/index.js` and cross-batch/control files remain I-owned; I integrates the reviewed health commit without independently rewriting its content.

